          "text": "{\"relation\":\"MATCH\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Nakedness primarily symbolizes shameful exposure in sin and the absence of divine or righteous covering.\",\"book_summary\":\"Nakedness means being exposed in one’s sin because one lacks covenant covering; garment is its opposite.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Sinful exposure without divine covering.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Sinful exposure without covenant covering.\",\"core_relation\":\"EQUIVALENT\",\"extension_relation\":\"SAME_CASES\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"No material boundary disagreement. The book specifies the covering as covenantal, but this fits the consensus concept of divine covering rather than excluding a clearly recognized class of cases. Its omission of other contextual meanings does not amount to denying them.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"None. Both identify nakedness with exposure in sin and the need for God-given covering.\",\"rationale\":\"The book’s covenantal wording is a concise theological specification of the consensus identification, not a replacement of its referent. The consensus repeatedly connects post-Fall nakedness with exposed sin, shame, and lack of righteous covering, while the book expresses the same core through covenant imagery.\",\"citation_support\":\"STRONG\",\"strongest_objection\":\"One could treat “without covenant covering” as a material narrowing because the consensus also recognizes non-covenantal contexts such as poverty, captivity, innocence, and bodily vulnerability. However, those are contextual alternatives rather than part of the minimum primary core being compared, and the book does not explicitly reject them.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
